    fix(arrival): make the ledger's prose speak the column's own vocabulary
    
    Three row notes in `domain/capability-topology.json` landed still reasoning in
    the numbering the same atom had just retired: "the binding's own arrival is L0",
    "L1, not L2, and the difference is byte-derived", "so this stays L1". Every
    `arrival` value moved mechanically with the rename; the free text did not,
    because nothing read it.
    
    That is clause A4's own shape -- a documented claim whose subject no longer
    carries it -- inside the ledger that names A4, so the cure is a reader rather
    than a careful pass. `check_topology` now reds any row note containing an
    `L<digit>` token, scoped to row notes: SKILL.md and README.md name the retired
    numbering deliberately, explaining why it is gone.
    
    Falsified against the bytes that actually landed, not against a synthetic
    mutation: the three notes from `main@de51956` restored into this tree produce
    
        topology row 'noodles-checkout-ceremony-binding' note still names the retired numbering ['L0']
        topology row 'sc-context-closure-engineering-checks' note still names the retired numbering ['L1', 'L2']
        topology row 'arrival-engineering-island-audit' note still names the retired numbering ['L1']
    
    so the gate catches exactly the escape it was written for. It also has its own
    planted hollowing in the validator selftest and its own case in `evals/cases.json`.
    
    The cce row's note now carries the second, stronger derivation as well: it is
    EXERCISED not because a cadence receipt's `checks=15` was reasoned about once,
    but because the row carries no `run`-kind receipt and the arrival column must
    equal what its receipts support.
    
    Receipt re-stamped through `gen_admission.py` over the changed tree. All four
    validators from `origin/main` run with `--root` against this candidate: PASS.
    
    Refs ed3c/skill-concerns#73

